1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
We talked about one-shot and few-shot examples in a previous lesson.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Now we're going to dig deeper into how to design effective few-shot examples.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,000
More examples isn't a win like people would expect.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
The real skill is choosing how many and making each one pull its weight.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
This lesson covers designing a small, diverse set that actually steers behavior.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Let's start by naming the three modes, because the choice among them is deliberate, not a ladder.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Zero-shot means you give the task with no worked examples at all,

8
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
leaning on the model's pre-trained knowledge and a clear instruction.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
For common, well-defined tasks, that's often enough.

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
One-shot means you provide exactly one input-output example.

11
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
It fits cases where a single, canonical example

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
fully defines the pattern and a second would add nothing.

13
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Multi-shot, which people also call few-shot,

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,000
means two or more examples that demonstrate the format, the tone, or the reasoning you want.

15
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Here's the part to hold on to.

16
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Diversity and quality of those examples matter far more than how many you cram in.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Now, why do I keep saying this isn't a fallback ladder?

18
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Because people treat zero-shot as the weak option

19
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,000
and reach for examples the moment output wobbles.

20
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:20,000
That's backwards.

21
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,000
A clear zero-shot instruction can match few-shot on common tasks,

22
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
and it stays leaner and cheaper.

23
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:31,000
The right way to think about it is matching the mode to the job.

24
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
How complex is the task, and how specific does the output format have to be?

25
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:42,000
If the format is subtle or the reasoning is easy to get wrong, examples earn their place.

26
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,000
If a plain instruction already lands it,

27
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
adding examples just burns tokens and can even lock in a pattern you didn't want.

28
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Add examples when they improve coverage, not out of reflex.

29
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Let's compare two ways to build a set.

30
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Picture a big pile of examples that all look basically the same.

31
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:06,000
It feels thorough, but it teaches Claude one narrow pattern,

32
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,000
and the prompt overfits to that happy path.

33
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Feed it something outside the pile, and it stumbles.

34
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Now picture a small set of well-chosen examples that deliberately span the input range. Easy cases. Tricky cases.

35
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,000
The odd shapes you actually see in production.

36
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:25,000
That smaller set steers far more reliably,

37
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
because Claude learns the boundaries of the behavior, not just the center.

38
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
So, when you're tempted to add a tenth near-duplicate, stop.

39
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Quality and coverage beat raw count every single time.

40
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
A few good examples outperform a heap of mediocre ones.

41
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:44,000
So what makes an example strong? Three marks.

42
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
First, relevant.

43
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Each example should mirror your actual use case closely,

44
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
so the pattern Claude picks up is the one you genuinely need, not a near miss.

45
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:57,000
Second, diverse.

46
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Your examples should vary enough to cover edge cases,

47
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
because if they're too similar,

48
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,000
Claude latches onto some accidental feature they share and generalizes the wrong thing.

49
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Third, structured.

50
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Each example needs to be clearly separated from your instructions,

51
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:16,000
so the model reads it as a demonstration to imitate,

52
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
not as a fresh directive to follow.

53
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Relevant, diverse, structured.

54
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Run any example set against those three, and the weak spots show up fast.

55
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Here's the trap that catches almost everyone, and it's worth slowing down on.

56
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
When you write examples, the easy inputs come to mind first,

57
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
so your set ends up all clean, all happy path.

58
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
The problem is that examples don't just show the model what good looks like.

59
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:46,000
They quietly tell it what to expect.

60
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:53,000
A set that only shows tidy inputs teaches Claude to assume every input is tidy,

61
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:55,000
and it falls apart on the messy ones.

62
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
So deliberately reach for the hard cases.

63
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:07,000
Include boundary inputs, an empty ticket body, a 40-page paste, a request that reads two ways.

64
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Include adversarial inputs, the ones designed to trip it up,

65
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
and show how each of those should be handled.

66
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
Cover the cases that actually break things.

67
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Let's turn that into a checklist of failure modes.

68
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Four ways an example set goes wrong.

69
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
One, too few and too alike.

70
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
A handful of near duplicates teaches a single narrow pattern,

71
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,000
and leaves real variety uncovered.

72
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
Two, happy path only.

73
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Clean inputs are all the model ever sees, so it never learns what a hard case looks like.

74
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Three, buried in the instructions.

75
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:49,000
If examples blur into your directions with no clear boundaries,

76
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
Claude may read them as commands instead of demonstrations.

77
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Four, stale or off target.

78
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Examples that drift from the current task or from the model you're now using

79
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
quietly steer toward outdated behavior.

80
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Scan your set for these four before you ship it.

81
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:12,000
Let's fix that third failure mode directly, because it's the easiest to get right.

82
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Structure is what lets Claude tell your examples, apart from your instructions.

83
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:22,000
The clean way to do it is to wrap each example in its own labeled tags,

84
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,000
and then wrap the whole group in an outer tag.

85
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
Now there's an unmistakable boundary.

86
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,000
These are demonstrations, not orders,

87
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,000
on how many three to five examples hits the sweet spot for most tasks,

88
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
enough to establish the pattern without bloating the prompt.

89
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
And you don't have to build the set alone.

90
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
You can ask Claude to critique your examples for relevance and diversity,

91
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
or to draft a few more to fill gaps you missed.

92
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Clear boundaries keep demonstrations from reading as new orders.

93
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Before we wrap, three techniques that take a decent set further.

94
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
First, show the reasoning, not just the answer.

95
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:07,000
If you put the thinking steps inside your examples, Claude generalizes the reasoning pattern,

96
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
which matters a lot for tasks that need a chain of logic.

97
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Not just a final output.

98
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Second, let Claude help.

99
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Hand it your set and ask it to evaluate the examples for relevance and diversity,

100
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:21,000
or to generate more.

101
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,000
It's a fast way to pressure test your coverage.

102
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
Third, stay canonical.

103
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Rather than stuffing in every edge case you can imagine,

104
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:35,000
curate a few diverse canonical examples that cleanly portray the behavior you want.

105
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
A tight representative set beats an exhaustive one.

106
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:42,000
Here's what to take away.

107
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:48,000
A few diverse, well-chosen examples steer Claude better than a large pile of lookalikes.

108
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Match the shots to the task.

109
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Cover the hard cases.

110
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
And wrap each example as a demonstration.

111
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Just remember, quality and diversity beats example count.

112
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
That covers designing effective few-shot examples.

113
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
When you're ready, I'll see you in the next one.

